ntals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Kentucky remains one of the most Republican-leaning states in presidential politics, with a partisan lean of roughly fifteen points toward the GOP. With the incumbent president term-limited, the race is open, and no candidate-specific polling or expert ratings are available for the state. The forecast therefore rests on the state's fundamental lean, which points to a solid but not insurmountable Republican advantage.\n\nRecent coverage highlights a potential Democratic surge into traditionally Republican states and the rising national profile of Kentucky's own governor, Andy Beshear, whose popularity has climbed in early polling. Those signals could narrow the gap if the national environment breaks strongly for Democrats, but without Kentucky-specific evidence they warrant only a modest adjustment. The state remains likely Republican, with meaningful uncertainty about how a national wave could reshape the margin.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0468, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1024, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.09, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.1001, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.1139, "margin": -13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.083, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.1075, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.0972, "margin": -14.5}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.0925, "margin": -14.5}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.1205, "margin": -13.0}]}